00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.130
questions from you all.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.070
So we are all
dedicated to making

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.860
this a welcoming
environment for everybody

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.260
and love to hear from
everyone no matter

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:04.300
your level of experience or
background, or area of work.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:10.420
It is also absolutely OK to
just listen in if you want to,

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.720
but there's also three ways you
can ask questions or provide

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.760
your perspective too.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.330
So one, you can always jump in
by raising your hand on Zoom

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.940
and I'll be watching
out for that.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.160
Two, you can put questions
into the Zoom chat.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.580
And feel free to just put
a little star next to it

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.710
if you want me to read it.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.080
And I can call on you to
introduce yourself and add

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.430
some context otherwise.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.610
And then third, we
have a Slido link

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.400
where you can ask
questions anonymously.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.910
And Tyler already shared
that in the chat right now.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.050
And so we do share the
recordings of each session

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.520
to the Posit YouTube.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.720
So you can always go back and
rewatch or share with a friend.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.190
But with all of that,
welcome everybody.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.620
Thank you so much
for joining us.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.160
And thank you so much Nick for
joining us, the co-host today.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.100
Nick Pylypiw is
Chief Data Officer

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.470
at Cape Fear Collective.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.949
And Nick, I'd love to have you
introduce yourself and maybe

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:11.890
start off by sharing a little
bit about your role and also

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:15.130
something you like to
do outside of work.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:15.940
Sure.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.245
So my name is Nick Pylypiw.

00:02:19.245 --> 00:02:21.370
I'm the Chief Data Officer
at Cape Fear Collective.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.648
We are, yep, Cape Fear movie.

00:02:22.648 --> 00:02:24.940
People are always like, is
it Cape Fear like the movie?

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.830
It is based on--

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:32.980
so Cape Fear region is that
southeastern North Carolina

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:37.390
region, Wilmington, if you're
familiar with that area.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.420
We are nonprofit based
out of Wilmington

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.930
that's really focused on the
state of North Carolina now.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.900
And we're really
looking to use data

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.090
to move the needle on
these social impact issues,

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.520
social determinants of health.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.410
My background is more in private
sector data science consulting.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.050
So I worked for a
couple of consultants

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.870
and was doing work
with Southwest Airlines

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:08.170
and Proctor &amp;amp; Gamble, and
Lowe's, and Baltimore Ravens,

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.530
and Cincinnati Reds, and a
bunch of different organizations

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:14.680
and really looked at
this as an opportunity

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:20.590
to make an impact in the state
that I'm from and that I love.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.390
Some things that
I do outside data

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.480
is just building
stuff in my yard.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.140
I'm building a porch
now, screened in porch in

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.750
the back of my house.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:36.400
I'm into music and surfing
and just hanging out here

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.480
in coastal North Carolina.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:41.110
Nice.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.320
Well, thank you so much,
Nick, for joining us.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.380
And it's really
impressive to hear

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.660
your journey and all
the different types

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.080
of organizations that
you've worked in.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.590
I know from your bio, you
were once a teacher as well.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:01.690
And I'm curious to hear a
little bit about that journey

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:04.270
into data science.

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:04.810
Yeah.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:08.920
So I did get my undergraduate.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.710
I got an undergraduate in
math and math education.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.910
It was a double--

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:16.690
dual major and taught high
school math for a couple

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:20.380
of years, geometry
and AP Statistics.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:23.200
And found myself
really every day

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.690
in class with these
AP Statistics kids

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:28.450
talking to them about, here's
why statistics is so cool

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.950
and here's all the awesome
things you can do with this

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.818
and the applications
and realize, of course,

00:04:33.818 --> 00:04:34.610
they're like, yeah.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:35.220
All right.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.630
Whatever Mr. P.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.000
And really realized that
I was talking myself

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.570
into going back to grad school.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.712
So I came home one
day and told my wife.

00:04:43.712 --> 00:04:45.670
I was like, I think I
want to go back to school

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:48.980
and get my master's
in data science.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:52.030
So I went back,
left the classroom,

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.350
went back to the
NC State Institute

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:00.040
for Advanced Analytics and
got my master's degree there

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:04.150
and then went into the
data science world.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.520
And I haven't really
looked back since.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.640
I still miss teaching
sometimes, but I

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.730
get to do a lot of teaching
in my leadership roles

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:14.240
in my organizations as well.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.960
So I've got a lot of
junior devs and I've

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.430
got six data scientists on my
team at Cape Fear Collective.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.390
I've had a number of interns
and volunteers over the years

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:28.990
that I still get to scratch that
itch for teaching a little bit

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:30.940
just outside the classroom.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.010
I was just going
to ask you what

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.550
does that teaching look like
now within your organization,

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.600
like helping new users learn.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:43.620
Yeah.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:49.550
Code review, just scaffolding.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:51.340
A lot of the things--

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.400
I like to say that I can
teach anybody to code.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:01.900
What I can't teach people
to do is be curious

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.860
and think about the
kinds of things they

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:07.248
should be asking on a project.

00:06:07.248 --> 00:06:09.790
So a lot of times in a project,
especially with a junior dev,

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.700
I'm showing them
my thought process.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.682
I'm thinking out loud and--

00:06:15.682 --> 00:06:18.400
a lot of times they'll--

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.680
Hey, I've got this error
and I can't figure this out.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.850
It's like, all right,
let's screen share it.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.450
It's like, all right, first
of all, what's the error said?

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:24.250
Well, I didn't look at it yet.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.167
All right, well, that's
the first thing to do,

00:06:26.167 --> 00:06:27.880
is look at the error.

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:31.433
Because these things sometimes
tell you exactly what's wrong.

00:06:31.433 --> 00:06:32.350
Sometimes they're not.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.710
Some of the errors are
less intuitive, especially

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.540
depending on
programming language.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:41.380
But we are an R shop so we
do a lot of code review.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:49.280
And a lot of it's just, what
are you expecting it to do?

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.983
Is this what you
expected to happen when

00:06:50.983 --> 00:06:52.150
you wrote this line of code?

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:55.100
It's like, well, no.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:59.270
So I think a lot of it is
just that modeling my thought

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:01.880
process out loud
for them to see,

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.160
here's how someone who's
been doing this for a while

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.820
thinks about these things and
approaches these solutions.

00:07:10.602 --> 00:07:12.060
I feel like I
have a lot of power

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.670
here when I get to ask these
questions in the beginning

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.860
as we're waiting for
them all to come in.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.600
But just a reminder to
anybody who just joined,

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:23.100
you can also use Slido if
you want to ask anonymously

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.320
or can raise your
hand here on Zoom

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.260
or put it in the Zoom chat.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:30.570
But Nick, something
I wanted to ask you

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.390
is, I know you've worked at
a few different Fortune 500

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.910
companies and you
said you've helped

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:40.090
them transform the way they
think about their customer

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.290
strategy.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.290
And I would love to
learn a little bit

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.240
about how you did that.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.090
I think a lot of people
here in the audience

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.520
might be in a
position where they're

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.510
trying to get their company
to be more data-driven

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:57.200
and trying to push
new tools forward.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.980
So that would be really helpful.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:00.790
Yeah, sure.

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:07.600
So as I'm sure you all have
had someone else tell you,

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.210
or maybe you haven't,
but a lot of people

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:14.680
are fans of analytics
and data science

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.770
if it's confirming what
they already believe.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:20.560
And one of the things working in
pricing and marketing analytics

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.590
at Lowe's, if I went into a
room with a bunch of people who

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:26.380
are marketing lumber and I said,
hey, the promotion that you

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.773
did last week did really well.

00:08:28.773 --> 00:08:30.190
They're all like,
yeah, all right.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:32.179
We really like this--
we really like this guy.

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:33.929
And if I was like,
hey, but it missed

00:08:33.929 --> 00:08:36.429
these markets and these markets,
then there is a reason why.

00:08:36.429 --> 00:08:38.080
No, you didn't analyze it right.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:39.580
You didn't do this
and you did that.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:43.000
So people like
data and analytics

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.840
if it confirms their
preconceived biases

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:48.070
and beliefs.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.530
But if it counters
that, then that's

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.540
where they start poking
holes and wanting

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:57.170
to find fault in it.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:59.980
So one thing that I've been
very successful in my career

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:05.380
is starting to build that trust.

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:07.600
Find some quick wins.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.060
What are some small projects
we can work on together

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:13.510
that are low stakes
projects that

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:15.130
gets people to buy
into and see like,

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:21.530
OK, now I can see why there's
value in this way of thinking.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:25.270
So that when that inevitable--

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:25.832
exactly.

00:09:25.832 --> 00:09:27.040
Don't start with forecasting.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:29.880
That's good advice.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.410
When that inevitable
cognitive dissonance comes,

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:39.885
then you've built up some
trust and some capital

00:09:39.885 --> 00:09:40.635
with that partner.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:44.760
That's a great point.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.468
Patrick, I see you
have your hand raised.

00:09:46.468 --> 00:09:47.532
You want to jump in?

00:09:47.532 --> 00:09:48.990
PATRICK S. TENNANT:
Thanks, Rachel.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:50.952
And yeah, good
morning, everybody.

00:09:50.952 --> 00:09:52.410
Happy to chat with
you again, Nick.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:54.690
You and I spoke a
couple of years ago.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.218
You may not remember,
but you were

00:09:56.218 --> 00:09:57.760
helpful on a couple
of things for me.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.700
And so I appreciate that
and glad to see you again.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:06.840
I'm wondering about the
comparison and contrast,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:10.740
similarities as well, of working
in those big corporate settings

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:14.430
versus where you are now in
nonprofit and social services

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:15.450
and that kind of thing.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:18.450
Personally, I've been in
academic and nonprofit settings

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:19.150
my whole career.

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.828
And so it seems to me-- it seems
appealing that people really

00:10:22.828 --> 00:10:24.870
have their things together
in a corporate setting

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.780
and everything is
well-funded and run smoothly.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:31.200
And I assume you can
dispel that myth for me,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.510
but I'm just wondering
about your thoughts on that.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:36.880
Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:38.300
And I think you
nailed part of it

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:42.640
and Marlene nailed a
part of it here too.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.820
Because there's certainly this--

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:50.410
it is a myth that these
large companies really have

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:51.850
everything all put together.

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:56.282
Because the reality is, I
saw some things in every--

00:10:56.282 --> 00:10:58.240
all these Fortune 500
companies I've worked in,

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.220
I saw things that would
make all of you cringe.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.170
And I'm probably not
going to say any of those

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:04.087
because I don't want to
get myself in trouble,

00:11:04.087 --> 00:11:06.240
but things that it's just
like, I can't believe you

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.240
run your stack that way.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.820
That is not the
way a $80 billion

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.115
company should be running that.

00:11:12.115 --> 00:11:15.610
This file should not reside
on one person's laptop.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.190
That should be in a shared
platform locked down,

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:20.230
et cetera.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.130
So there's that.

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:23.350
One of the biggest
differences, though,

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:26.860
for me is in large
companies, people can hide.

00:11:30.614 --> 00:11:36.040
There's room-- your errors and
your weak links on your teams

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:40.720
don't expose themselves as
readily as on small teams.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.450
On some of the small teams I've
been on-- my previous company

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:46.010
was 35 people.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:49.270
And currently at Cape Fear
Collective, we're a team of 12.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:53.710
And there's no room for
somebody to just not

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:56.500
be pulling their weight.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:58.970
So I think that's
a big difference.

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:02.158
Another big difference is I
think a lot of the Fortune 500

00:12:02.158 --> 00:12:04.450
companies, whether or not
they're actually implementing

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:09.040
it is another thing, but they at
least know that they should be.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:11.500
They've heard enough times
through all the articles

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:14.013
and through the conferences
and through their leadership

00:12:14.013 --> 00:12:15.430
like, you need to
be doing things,

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:17.903
data, KPIs, measurements,
dashboards, et cetera.

00:12:17.903 --> 00:12:19.570
They know they need
to be working on it.

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:21.400
There's some
pressure to do that.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:24.460
Whereas in the social space,
a lot of the stuff is very new

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:29.200
and it's a dramatic
cultural shift.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:31.510
A lot of the
nonprofits that we work

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:36.940
with are used to
being able to just--

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:39.615
and I'm going to say
this tough for a minute,

00:12:39.615 --> 00:12:41.740
they're used to just patting
themselves on the back

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.000
and saying like,
we did good work.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:43.958
And that's good enough.

00:12:43.958 --> 00:12:45.850
And the thing is
like, for the funders

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.840
and the philanthropic
organizations and grants that

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:51.840
are funding them, it's
not good enough anymore.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.590
You have to be able
to show us that you're

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:57.190
moving a needle, which means
you have to set some KPIs,

00:12:57.190 --> 00:13:01.160
measure them, collect data, and
show that you're making impact

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.160
against that or else
you're not going to qualify

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:04.452
for that next round of funding.

00:13:04.452 --> 00:13:08.402
We can't just do feel
good funding anymore.

00:13:08.402 --> 00:13:10.110
PATRICK S. TENNANT:
That's awesome, Nick.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:14.680
Thank you very much for sharing
and offering that perspective.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:15.660
Sure.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.720
I see a few anonymous
questions coming in on Slido.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.120
And one was, can you
talk a little bit

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.850
about getting your first
role in data science?

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:27.255
Any tips for jumping from data
analytics to data science?

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.280
Sure.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:35.310
And this whole analyst
versus data science thing

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.010
is such a weird
ambiguous thing anyways.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.530
If you ask, there's
people that I

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:43.020
know who call themselves
analysts or titles analysts

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:45.600
and they're programming
in Python at R every day

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.328
and it's like, that's
a data scientist to me.

00:13:48.328 --> 00:13:50.370
And then there's other
people who are like, well,

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:51.240
I'm an analyst.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.330
And it's like, well, are you a
business analyst or are you--

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:02.190
titles all get wonky anyways.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:06.810
But for me, getting
my first role,

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.150
I really wanted to
be somewhere where

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:12.090
I had other folks who
could challenge me

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:13.230
that I could learn from.

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:18.330
I think a mistake that I see
in a lot of young devs and data

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.780
scientists is they go
right to a startup or right

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:23.460
to a small company--
and there's nothing

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:25.410
wrong with either of those.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.310
I've done both-- but
you're on an island.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:31.170
You don't have the
support that you need.

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:35.500
And a lot of these--

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:36.860
I just saw the joke in the chat.

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:39.860
Yeah, that's pretty
accurate right there too.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:43.090
But a lot of these
larger companies

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:45.190
have support
structures in place.

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:48.730
There's going to be senior data
scientists, senior analysts who

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:52.280
can help you and know
the data sets and can--

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:55.360
so find one of those
large companies

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:59.860
with a solid structure, latch
on to a couple senior devs

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:01.600
and be a sponge.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:05.075
Just absorb everything
that you can.

00:15:05.075 --> 00:15:07.450
And then the other thing that
I can provide that I always

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:09.575
tell people who are trying
to break into this space

00:15:09.575 --> 00:15:12.040
is nobody likes
somebody who pretends

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:15.370
like they know everything
because none of us do.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.590
There's power in
saying, I don't know.

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:20.590
And if you apply
for a job on my team

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:23.320
and say that you know how
to do natural processing

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:28.210
languages modeling and I
put you on a project that

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:32.150
requires that skill set and it's
obvious that you don't, I'll

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:32.990
know.

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:36.680
You're not fooling-- you're
only fooling somebody until it's

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:38.070
time for you to use that skill.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:41.510
So be honest about what you
know and how good you are at it.

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:45.260
If you say you're at
10 in R, I don't even

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.290
consider myself a 10
in R. So you better

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:50.570
be really good if you say that.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:56.840
Marlene, that point
about every time

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:58.110
I think I'm an
expert in something,

00:15:58.110 --> 00:15:59.527
I think there's a
graph that shows

00:15:59.527 --> 00:16:01.450
that the more you
learn about something

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:03.950
and then you hit a point where
you're just like-- there have

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:05.575
been times in my
career where I'm like,

00:16:05.575 --> 00:16:08.060
I'm an expert Excel user.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:12.380
And then I'll meet somebody
that I work with I'm like, wow.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:13.533
I'm like a two.

00:16:13.533 --> 00:16:15.200
And then I'll be like,
OK, I've learned.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:16.790
Now I'm like a 7 or 8.

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:20.030
I'll meet someone else
I'm like, I'm a four.

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:23.900
So definitely-- there's
always someone out there

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:27.080
who's better at it than you.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:28.520
Thank you.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:31.130
Another anonymous
question was, any advice

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:34.280
for someone working for a
large corporation that's

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:38.685
very resistant to new tools to
progress in the data science

00:16:38.685 --> 00:16:39.185
world?

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:43.130
Yeah.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:48.170
Brush up your resume.

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:50.170
No.

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:54.562
I think it's a
hard thing moving--

00:16:54.562 --> 00:16:56.020
someone else made
the point earlier

00:16:56.020 --> 00:17:00.700
that big ships turn really
slowly or something like that.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:03.640
And it's true.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:09.640
Yeah, I think other than
just keep beating the drum

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:14.500
and keep trying to prove
value, unfortunately,

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:17.650
a lot of these companies
just sometimes it's

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.880
going to take way more
than you to get it.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:23.740
So what I do or what
I recommend to people

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.599
is when you're
interviewing for that job,

00:17:25.599 --> 00:17:27.670
make sure the
questions that you ask

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:30.582
are hinting at what
that data culture is.

00:17:30.582 --> 00:17:33.040
One of the questions that I
think is a really good question

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:39.960
to ask is, what is
a model or a process

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.540
that your team has
built that is currently

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:46.080
being used for the company
to make decisions on?

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.410
And you'll be really surprised
to hear the wide variety

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:50.670
of answers to that question.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.690
Because it really obvious to
hear someone's like fumbling.

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:55.440
Like, oh, we built
this thing but it never

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.070
got implemented
because of whatever.

00:17:57.070 --> 00:18:00.660
Or we've been working on this
thing for a couple of years

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:03.797
and it's never quite gotten
the traction that it needs.

00:18:03.797 --> 00:18:05.880
And there's going to be
other teams that are like,

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:10.080
oh, we've got this thing that's
helping with our search engine

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:12.270
optimization and we've got
this other model that's

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:17.250
out there that helps determine
where we build new locations.

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:21.268
And what you hear there
is, this is an organization

00:18:21.268 --> 00:18:23.310
that is putting their
money where their mouth is.

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:25.980
They're not only hiring
people to build these models,

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.830
but they're making business
decisions based off that.

00:18:29.830 --> 00:18:32.797
So I think-- yeah, I
don't really-- yeah.

00:18:32.797 --> 00:18:33.630
I'll leave it there.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:39.020
Thank you.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:41.390
Just to give us a little
bit more context to you

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:43.460
on some of the work that
your team does today,

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:45.470
I thought it might
be helpful to hear

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:48.290
a little bit about the
community data platform

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:55.020
or maybe an example of a
problem your team is solving.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:55.520
Sure.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:02.000
So one of the problems that we
hear a lot or one of the issues

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:04.550
that we hear a lot is
there's not data for that.

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.890
You have-- so
organizations are really

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:09.530
working to build data capacity
across these nonprofits.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:12.140
There's 1,200 nonprofits
in New Hanover County

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:14.840
and there's no
shortage of passion.

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:18.790
They all think they're doing
good work, and they are.

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:23.530
But there's no
real data capacity

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:27.190
to understand what's the value
in generating a hypothesis

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:31.000
and measuring data,
collecting data?

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.250
What we always hear
is I'd say, well, hey,

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:36.160
you've been doing this
thing for 30 years.

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:36.820
How's it going?

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:37.570
How is it working?

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:40.670
And they're like, well, people
really like our food boxes

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:41.170
or whatever.

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:43.060
It's like, OK, that's cool.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:47.680
Is it actually moving the needle
on food insecurity or hunger,

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.162
or health issues
in your community?

00:19:49.162 --> 00:19:51.370
They're like, well, there's
not really data for that.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:54.290
I'm like, oh, let's
see about that.

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:55.840
So one of the
first things we did

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:57.640
is we pulled in Census
Bureau data, Bureau

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:01.150
of Labor Statistics,
CDC, FHFA, and all

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:03.400
these publicly available
data sources that,

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:05.260
for us as data
scientists, are really

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:08.260
easy to grab because we know
how to use an API to go do this,

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:12.340
we know how to scrape a
website, we know how to search--

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:14.320
google specific terms
that are going to lead

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:16.960
us more successful results.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:19.933
But for the average
nonprofit community leader,

00:20:19.933 --> 00:20:21.350
they don't know
how to use an API.

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:22.267
They don't use Python.

00:20:22.267 --> 00:20:23.920
They don't know
any of this stuff.

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:26.050
So for the first time
ever in our community,

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:28.690
we really have this
holistic 360-degree view

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:31.690
of what a community looks like.

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:35.050
And the way we're using
that is to do things like,

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:37.945
you've got a nonprofit
who wants to do something,

00:20:37.945 --> 00:20:40.600
here, we're going to find
a metric that works for you

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:45.640
and help you build a
mission around that.

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:48.460
We are going to
decrease children

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:51.370
in concentrated poverty
in this neighborhood

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:57.490
by 5% over the next 10 years
and we need $200,000 to do that,

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:58.810
or whatever.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:01.630
That is a statement that
is impossible to make

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:04.660
without understanding how
many people currently live

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:07.420
in that situation, what
the current projection is

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:13.120
for that neighborhood, and
what are reasonable decreases

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:14.780
for that metric.

00:21:14.780 --> 00:21:17.860
So a lot of our work
is really around that,

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:21.070
but there's some
other larger projects.

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:22.180
One that I'll talk about--

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:25.270
I'm actually going to
drop this link in here,

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.850
is this awesome video
that we just put out.

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:29.110
It walks through this.

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:32.860
But the Michael
Jordan Foundation

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:36.580
just put these two
clinics in Wilmington.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:38.620
Michael Jordan's
from Wilmington.

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:41.225
And he wanted to put them
in areas where they're

00:21:41.225 --> 00:21:43.600
going to make the most impact
for low income marginalized

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:45.070
populations.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:46.810
The hospital system
said, that's great.

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:48.400
We know a lot about
chronic disease

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:50.150
and we know a lot about
health conditions.

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:53.890
We know very little about the
social determinants of health

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:55.180
that you've been studying.

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:59.500
So help us figure out, from a
data perspective, specifically

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:03.530
where these clinics are going
to make the most impact.

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:06.955
So that's our flagship
project and one

00:22:06.955 --> 00:22:08.080
that we're really proud of.

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:09.310
This is North Carolina.

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:15.460
That's a great video
to check out too.

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:16.700
Thank you.

00:22:16.700 --> 00:22:19.317
Morris, I see you had asked
a question in the chat

00:22:19.317 --> 00:22:19.900
a bit earlier.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:22.522
Do you want to jump in next?

00:22:22.522 --> 00:22:23.730
MORRIS JOHNSON: Yeah, Thanks.

00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:24.230
No.

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:28.230
I think he just answered
it, partially at least.

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:30.030
Yeah.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:33.270
I'm working at a nonprofit
and I'm a social worker.

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:35.370
Transformation is always
really the big question.

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:36.540
How do we measure that?

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:38.010
That's what we want.

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:40.930
We're pretty good
at tracking outputs,

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:44.130
but the outcomes and
it's a little bit more

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:46.620
difficult. So yeah.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:48.730
But these are helpful.

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:49.640
Thank you.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:50.140
Yeah.

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:52.830
And that's actually a question
that we struggle with a lot

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:54.480
too, so I'm glad
you brought that up.

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:58.050
Because a lot of times, it's
not fair to say, hey, food bank,

00:22:58.050 --> 00:23:02.520
it's on you, food bank
of New Hanover County,

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:07.380
to turn this area into a
non-food desert by yourself.

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:09.570
And not only that,
but the lag in

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:13.830
data is such that the USDA only
classifies these food deserts

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:14.770
every five years.

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:16.950
So it might be five,
10 years before we even

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:20.260
know if you've moved the
needle on this macro metric.

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:26.640
So what we try to do is we try
to tie these micro data metrics

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.470
to those leading indicators.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:32.070
So we might not know the Census
Bureau data for another two

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:33.600
years because of
the lag, but what

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:35.940
we do have is this real-time
emergency department

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:38.850
data or real-time police data
that we have for our region.

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.650
And if we can find
the correlation

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:43.860
between those things, then
what we can do is we can say,

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:47.700
we can see a signal here
at a right now level

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:50.850
that we know is tied
to this macro level

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:54.420
and then we can
hypothesize that you're

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:57.600
moving the needle at
this greater level

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:00.195
that you haven't been
able to measure before.

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:07.040
Another question that was
asked anonymously a bit earlier

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:10.760
was, how do nonprofits think
about the substantial costs

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:11.990
of a data team?

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:14.690
Are these getting more common
with the trend of needing

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:18.180
to prove moving the needle?

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:18.680
Yeah.

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:20.810
I mean, the reality
is it's unattainable

00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:24.050
for the average nonprofit to
have a data scientist on staff.

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:27.620
But that's OK because
that's one of the things

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:28.880
that we're hoping to do.

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:34.810
Because part of our business
model is we don't need--

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:37.430
Catholic charity missions of
Wilmington, North Carolina

00:24:37.430 --> 00:24:39.290
doesn't need a full-time
data scientist.

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:42.500
All they really need is
40 hours with somebody

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:47.250
on my team to help kick start
the way they think about data.

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:52.070
So instead of saying, hey, every
nonprofit in North Carolina

00:24:52.070 --> 00:24:54.330
needs to go hire
a data scientist,

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:56.880
it's, how do we
become that knowledge

00:24:56.880 --> 00:25:01.370
hub that through contracts,
through pro bono contracts--

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.860
over the last three years,
we've done $2.8 million

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:06.710
in pro bono data
science consulting.

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:09.020
So what that looks like
is somebody on my team

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:11.210
sitting right next
to a nonprofit,

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:13.910
looking at their sales force
or looking at their Excel

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:17.340
or downloading their
data and saying, hey,

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:21.620
did you know that
15% of your clients

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:24.710
are from this neighborhood,
but that actually

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:27.017
the majority of the need
in our county is over here?

00:25:27.017 --> 00:25:29.100
And they're like, never
really thought about that.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:29.933
It's like, OK, cool.

00:25:29.933 --> 00:25:31.850
So maybe like put
some flyers here.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:34.845
And let's try to make sure
that your services are--

00:25:34.845 --> 00:25:37.220
that's something that they'd
never really had the ability

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:41.200
to do before because turning
addresses into latitude

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:43.700
and longitude coordinates and
matching it with Census Bureau

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:47.870
data is out of reach for a
lot of them, all of them.

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:54.960
How did you start thinking
about the different data sets

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:56.910
that you might start using?

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:00.000
I'm trying to also
imagine for people

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.620
who are working
in a company too,

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:03.750
there's probably so much
data within their company

00:26:03.750 --> 00:26:07.125
that they don't know how
to use or where to get.

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:13.500
Yeah.

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.720
I mean, I wish I had a
better answer for it,

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:18.540
but the reality is I just
started grabbing stuff.

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:23.250
I like to say I've never
met a data set in this space

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:25.330
that I didn't like.

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:26.250
And that's still true.

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:28.020
Even though we've met
some garbage data sets,

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.478
we still bring it
all in because we

00:26:29.478 --> 00:26:34.860
think that the triangulation
and the whole combination of all

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:39.570
these different data sets
really adds to the story.

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:42.520
And I'll give you
an example of this.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:47.040
We're talking to a hospital
in New Hanover County.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:50.610
And I did see a question
about, is it just

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:52.065
Eastern North Carolina?

00:26:52.065 --> 00:26:54.803
We're across the state
now, but we started there.

00:26:54.803 --> 00:26:56.220
So majority of the
work that we do

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:58.140
is still Eastern North Carolina.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:26:59.910
But we had a hospital
who was looking

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:02.760
at doing some outreach
for COVID testing

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:04.260
and they were looking
at where do we

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:06.660
need to put the Spanish fliers?

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:08.040
We've got these English fliers.

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:08.970
We've got TV ads.

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:09.780
We've got signs.

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:13.560
Where do we need to put fliers
that are written in Spanish?

00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:18.800
And we told them, well,
you need to put them here.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:21.710
And they're like, well,
based on the Census Bureau

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:23.960
data, only like--

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:25.110
I'm making the numbers up.

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:25.820
I can't remember off
the top of my head.

00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:27.830
But only like 5%
of the people there

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:31.070
are Hispanic or English
second language, or whatever.

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:33.650
And it's like, OK,
that might be true,

00:27:33.650 --> 00:27:37.250
but for reasons that we don't
have to discuss in this call,

00:27:37.250 --> 00:27:38.990
a lot of times
non-English speakers

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:41.212
are de-incentivized to
fill out the Census Bureau.

00:27:41.212 --> 00:27:42.920
And actually, there's
a fear factor there

00:27:42.920 --> 00:27:43.753
and everything else.

00:27:43.753 --> 00:27:46.820
So the Census Bureau
historically undercounts them,

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:50.150
number one, and then, number
two, based on your data,

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:54.830
you have a large portion of
Spanish speaking patients

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:57.140
who live in this area.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:59.480
So there's a mismatch
between the Census Bureau

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:02.240
data and the hospital data.

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:03.080
Who's right?

00:28:03.080 --> 00:28:04.940
Who's wrong?

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:07.130
Don't know, but it's
like, if you only

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:09.770
had one of those data sets, you
might make a decision that's

00:28:09.770 --> 00:28:11.100
not actually the right one.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:14.883
So by having them both, you
have a more complete version

00:28:14.883 --> 00:28:15.425
of the story.

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:20.250
Thank you.

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:23.240
Elaine, I know Nick just
answer the first part

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:25.610
of that question, but would
love to have you jump in

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:27.695
and ask the second part.

00:28:30.388 --> 00:28:31.180
ELAINE MCVEY: Yeah.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:33.610
So I'm just-- I'm curious
because it sounds like you're

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:36.070
regionally focused
with this model

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:43.160
of a combination of contract
and pro bono central support.

00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:46.090
How does that work in terms
of getting to know people,

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:51.190
helping them identify needs and
so on in your mind versus maybe

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:55.390
focusing on a particular
type of nonprofit

00:28:55.390 --> 00:29:01.095
and working in a larger
national international scope?

00:29:01.095 --> 00:29:01.595
Yeah.

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:06.090
It was really natural for us.

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.480
We had some really early
success in the first year

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:09.860
where people were--

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:12.730
in New Hanover County, and
that's that Cape Fear region.

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:13.770
Like we love this.

00:29:13.770 --> 00:29:14.390
We love it.

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:17.843
We started seeing like, all
of a sudden people in Raleigh

00:29:17.843 --> 00:29:20.010
are asking us and people
in Charlotte are asking us.

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:21.960
And we're like, are we
going to go statewide?

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:23.680
Are we going to go regional
if the whole Southeast

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:24.330
in North Carolina?

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:26.038
Are we going to be
like Eastern Seaboard?

00:29:26.038 --> 00:29:27.480
When does this stop?

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.630
And it auto-corrected
itself because we

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.220
got to the mountains
of North Carolina

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:34.630
and people up there all of
a sudden were just like,

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:38.280
I don't care where you're from
in Eastern North Carolina.

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:40.500
You don't know anything
about us up here.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:45.360
So there still has to be this
boots on the ground connection

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:46.410
to the region or else--

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:49.860
For the same reasons we
talked about building trust

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:52.300
and capital in a
Fortune 500 company,

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:54.360
if you think about the
state in that same way,

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:57.330
if you're from the
beach town and you

00:29:57.330 --> 00:29:59.640
try to go up into the
mountains and tell them

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:02.177
what they don't know about
their public health crises,

00:30:02.177 --> 00:30:04.260
you're going to get run
out of there with a stick.

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:08.550
So I mean, you still have
to build these connections

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:11.520
organically.

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:14.760
That's limited our
business plan in a way

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.310
that I think makes sense,
not in a bad way like, oh,

00:30:17.310 --> 00:30:19.600
we can't do that.

00:30:19.600 --> 00:30:24.180
Now, the other thing is
we've made it very clear

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:28.770
that we want to work by, with,
and through, and not around.

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:30.512
So areas that already have--

00:30:30.512 --> 00:30:32.220
if there's an area in
North Carolina that

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:34.135
has an organization
that does what we do,

00:30:34.135 --> 00:30:35.760
we definitely don't
want to go in there

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:38.160
and say like, we're
going to replace you.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:40.680
It's how do we work together?

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:45.480
How do we start sharing some
tools on some of this work

00:30:45.480 --> 00:30:50.160
because we understand
that you're here and we

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:52.560
can't-- there's plenty of
work for all of us anyways.

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:56.310
So we don't really have
our sights set on any--

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:59.040
there's so much work in
Eastern North Carolina.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:00.420
Here's a stat for you.

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:05.550
If you carved North Carolina
in half down Highway I-95

00:31:05.550 --> 00:31:07.770
and you just looked
at the East part of it

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:10.650
and made that a state, it
would be the poorest state

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:12.410
in the country.

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:14.965
So no Raleigh, no Charlotte, no
Winston-Salem, no Greensboro.

00:31:14.965 --> 00:31:16.840
If you just looked at
East part of the state,

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:18.715
it would be the poorest
state in the country.

00:31:18.715 --> 00:31:21.310
So I mean, that's the
level of challenge

00:31:21.310 --> 00:31:22.310
that we're working with.

00:31:22.310 --> 00:31:24.490
So we have our hands
full with just that,

00:31:24.490 --> 00:31:27.910
but definitely are opening
our eyes to the whole state.

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:35.712
Bill, I see--

00:31:35.712 --> 00:31:36.920
Did I answer your question?

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:38.390
I'm sorry.

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:40.880
I think I did.

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:43.400
ELAINE MCVEY: Yeah,
I think that aspect

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:47.480
of working with people
who trust you and trust

00:31:47.480 --> 00:31:52.137
that you know something about
their domain is a good--

00:31:52.137 --> 00:31:52.970
that's a good point.

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:54.440
That makes sense.

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:57.290
And it makes me laugh because
I know North Carolina.

00:31:57.290 --> 00:31:59.105
Also makes sense
from that regard.

00:32:03.370 --> 00:32:05.107
Bill, I think you
asked the question

00:32:05.107 --> 00:32:06.190
in the chat a bit earlier.

00:32:06.190 --> 00:32:07.357
Do you want to jump in next?

00:32:10.960 --> 00:32:14.050
And I can read the
question for you.

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:17.090
It was, Nick, maybe you
stated this earlier,

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:19.420
but are you using
cloud-based resources

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:22.720
or on-prem infrastructure?

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:25.600
And then the second part
is, have you grown your own

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:29.920
or do you use Posit or some
other service provider?

00:32:29.920 --> 00:32:30.420
Yeah.

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:33.820
So we're using-- like I
said, we are an R shop.

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:37.200
So we're using R
Shiny in Shiny apps.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:39.120
The community data
platform that we've built

00:32:39.120 --> 00:32:43.830
is a MySQL based
product that is in AWS.

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:45.990
So we're using S3.

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:50.110
So a lot of the stuff that
we're using is cloud-based.

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:54.330
And we just made that decision
early on because it was just

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:55.770
so much easier to scale it.

00:32:55.770 --> 00:32:58.740
And cloud storage
is so cheap now

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:02.740
that it just doesn't
really make any sense.

00:33:02.740 --> 00:33:07.080
So definitely excited to
see about the changes that

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:07.855
come with Posit.

00:33:07.855 --> 00:33:10.230
And I'm not just saying that
because this is a Posit call

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:12.330
because, I mean, I'm an R guy.

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:14.700
I love R. I've used Python.

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:15.360
I've used SAS.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:18.570
I actually started
my career in SAS

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:23.340
and quickly switched
to something else.

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:27.650
But yeah, that's
what we're using.

00:33:27.650 --> 00:33:28.490
Thank you.

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:31.440
Marlene, I see you have a
question in the chat too.

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:33.920
Do you want to jump in?

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:34.610
Oh, sure.

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:35.370
Hi.

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:35.870
Hi, Nick.

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:37.670
Hi, everyone.

00:33:37.670 --> 00:33:38.818
Love your talk so far.

00:33:38.818 --> 00:33:40.610
I don't think there
has been a single one I

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:41.790
haven't enjoyed this.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:45.410
I feel repetitive every time
I get a chance to say that.

00:33:45.410 --> 00:33:47.870
I'm always curious.

00:33:47.870 --> 00:33:50.420
You seem very articulate
and obviously you

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:54.230
know your stuff with analytics,
but I'm always curious,

00:33:54.230 --> 00:33:57.020
is there a skill set
or talent that you

00:33:57.020 --> 00:34:00.110
think most people wouldn't
associate with analysts?

00:34:00.110 --> 00:34:03.113
Like a preconceived
notion about we're

00:34:03.113 --> 00:34:05.030
just nerds that sit in
cubicles and don't know

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:06.590
how to interact with people.

00:34:06.590 --> 00:34:10.070
What's a talent or
skill set that you

00:34:10.070 --> 00:34:12.380
think people should
probably know, it's probably

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:14.239
handy to have, that
maybe it served you well

00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:19.219
that we wouldn't think of
as analysts and scientists?

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:21.830
Yeah.

00:34:21.830 --> 00:34:23.870
I think it's going
to sound cliche,

00:34:23.870 --> 00:34:26.480
but just like
listening and being

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:27.949
able to have a conversation.

00:34:27.949 --> 00:34:29.900
I can't hire robots.

00:34:29.900 --> 00:34:32.340
There's no position
on my team for robots.

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:35.719
And these days of we
keep the nerds over there

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:37.815
and we give them their
assignment under the door,

00:34:37.815 --> 00:34:38.690
and don't touch them.

00:34:38.690 --> 00:34:39.290
Leave them alone.

00:34:39.290 --> 00:34:40.290
Let them do their thing.

00:34:40.290 --> 00:34:41.300
Those days are over.

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:44.900
There's no space for
somebody who does that.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:50.480
And most companies, maybe
some very specific niche

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:52.110
tech spaces there are.

00:34:52.110 --> 00:34:59.180
But in general, listening and
being able to go back and take

00:34:59.180 --> 00:35:02.660
feedback and not be offended.

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:04.940
Because a lot of
times, there's this--

00:35:04.940 --> 00:35:08.180
I was actually just
laughing at this meme

00:35:08.180 --> 00:35:12.568
I saw on LinkedIn
where it was like how

00:35:12.568 --> 00:35:13.735
people see a data scientist.

00:35:13.735 --> 00:35:15.618
And it's a bunch of
people sneering over

00:35:15.618 --> 00:35:17.660
at this alien who's typing
in a foreign language.

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:21.350
And it was like, how data
scientists see everyone else.

00:35:21.350 --> 00:35:23.360
And it's like they're
all like cavemen

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:25.602
and they're all hank
waving these clubs around.

00:35:25.602 --> 00:35:27.560
And there's some truth
in that, and it's awful.

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:30.470
But it's like, there's
been so many times

00:35:30.470 --> 00:35:33.780
where I've got out of a
meeting and somebody said like,

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:35.780
oh, well, they don't
really know what they want.

00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:37.530
I'm going to build
them something anyways.

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:40.700
It's like, OK, that's a
route that you can go.

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:43.460
That's a possibility, but
where does that get you?

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:46.970
That gets you now you've
spent a whole sprint

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:48.560
making a development
that you know

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:50.480
they just said they don't want.

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:53.917
And now you're going to
be frustrated and double

00:35:53.917 --> 00:35:56.250
down and say, now they really
don't know what they want,

00:35:56.250 --> 00:35:58.292
but it was really just
because she didn't listen.

00:35:58.292 --> 00:36:03.330
So taking those extra
moments to really listen

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:05.810
to what they're saying
and not-- and there's

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:07.790
a difference between
listening to the question

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:09.950
they're really
asking and finding

00:36:09.950 --> 00:36:12.950
the question you want them to be
asking and what they're saying.

00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:18.900
And that's a subtle difference
that I think needs to be made.

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:19.400
Listening.

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:19.900
Cool.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:23.410
Thanks for that.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:25.350
Have you had to
coach people on that

00:36:25.350 --> 00:36:30.590
or any tips for us on that?

00:36:30.590 --> 00:36:36.860
Yeah, definitely had to
coach some people on that.

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:40.880
I think a lot of times
it's just I definitely

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:44.047
have the management style of
just modeling and scaffolding

00:36:44.047 --> 00:36:44.630
that behavior.

00:36:47.180 --> 00:36:49.460
I feel like it's easier
for people to learn it

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:52.670
if they just see
interacting with clients

00:36:52.670 --> 00:36:55.220
in a respectful way
and listening to them

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:58.400
and asking those
probing questions.

00:36:58.400 --> 00:37:00.570
Because you can
just say like, hey,

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:02.900
make sure you're listening
to the client and make sure

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:05.060
you're really asking
those probing questions.

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:09.300
But it's like, what
does that look like?

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:10.350
Absolutely.

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:12.540
Miles, I see you have
a question in the chat.

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:15.120
You want to jump in?

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:16.080
Sure.

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:16.580
Hey, Nick.

00:37:16.580 --> 00:37:17.790
Thanks for doing this.

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:21.860
So when you were talking about
the difference between East

00:37:21.860 --> 00:37:24.050
Carolina and the
coastal Carolinas

00:37:24.050 --> 00:37:27.260
and how do you organically build
those relationships because I

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:29.780
feel like maybe there
would be maybe not

00:37:29.780 --> 00:37:33.140
resentment but some
standoffishness of like,

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:34.370
OK, this is a coastal guy.

00:37:34.370 --> 00:37:35.130
He doesn't get it.

00:37:35.130 --> 00:37:36.463
He doesn't know what's going on.

00:37:36.463 --> 00:37:38.940
So how do you build that
trust and that relationship?

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:39.440
Yeah.

00:37:39.440 --> 00:37:41.120
And it's even worse
when they find out

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:43.103
I live in Raleigh
because I actually I

00:37:43.103 --> 00:37:45.020
grew up in Eastern North
Carolina on the coast

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:46.460
but now I'm in Raleigh
in the capital.

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:48.508
So then I'm talking to
some of these small towns

00:37:48.508 --> 00:37:51.050
and it's like, oh, just some
guy from route from the capital,

00:37:51.050 --> 00:37:53.315
from the city telling
us how to do stuff.

00:37:53.315 --> 00:37:54.860
If we ignore him
long enough, he'll

00:37:54.860 --> 00:37:56.600
go away and start
bugging somebody else.

00:37:59.105 --> 00:38:04.580
The same way that we talked
about building capital

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:07.370
in a big company.

00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:09.410
Especially with
this line of work,

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:11.840
you'd be surprised at
how much buy-in you

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:14.570
can get if you show
up and you actually

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:17.210
know how to pronounce the
town name and the county name

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:18.440
correctly.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:22.615
Do you go into Beaufort, North
Carolina and you say Buford.

00:38:22.615 --> 00:38:23.990
That's South
Carolina by the way.

00:38:23.990 --> 00:38:26.532
Spelled exactly the same, but
it's Beaufort in South Carolina

00:38:26.532 --> 00:38:28.032
and it's Beaufort
in North Carolina.

00:38:28.032 --> 00:38:29.930
If you go into Beaufort
and call it Beaufort,

00:38:29.930 --> 00:38:33.710
you've lost them before even
the first slides hit the screen

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:37.100
because it's just some
outsider coming in here

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:38.420
who knows nothing about us.

00:38:38.420 --> 00:38:40.760
So just being able to go
in there and say-- and one

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:43.850
of the things that our
data science team has done

00:38:43.850 --> 00:38:48.200
is when you're learning
about a new area, one

00:38:48.200 --> 00:38:50.780
of the first tasks that we've
started doing is put together

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:56.120
a deck of just, I want to know
the five worst areas of poverty

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:56.730
in this area.

00:38:56.730 --> 00:39:02.210
I want to know the five areas
where there's great employment

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:04.940
parity or great
employment rates, all

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:06.110
these different things.

00:39:06.110 --> 00:39:10.330
And through that
exploration, you understand,

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:12.830
oh, there's this town here and
there's this other town here,

00:39:12.830 --> 00:39:13.830
and this is this county.

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:15.120
And you learn about that area.

00:39:15.120 --> 00:39:17.390
So now when you are
in a conversation,

00:39:17.390 --> 00:39:19.415
you can intelligently
talk about that region.

00:39:19.415 --> 00:39:21.290
It doesn't sound like
you're just some person

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:23.120
looking at a chart
and saying like,

00:39:23.120 --> 00:39:24.830
you need to do this and this.

00:39:24.830 --> 00:39:27.350
That's really
helped us to really

00:39:27.350 --> 00:39:31.120
just learn about the areas
that we're working in.

00:39:31.120 --> 00:39:33.220
Thank you.

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:35.620
Thank you so much for that.

00:39:35.620 --> 00:39:38.237
Coulter, do you want
to jump in next?

00:39:38.237 --> 00:39:39.070
COULTER: Yeah, sure.

00:39:39.070 --> 00:39:40.120
Thanks.

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:42.280
Just a quick
background, I actually

00:39:42.280 --> 00:39:45.580
work on a data science team in
my local public health agency

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:49.180
and we quite often
help out nonprofits.

00:39:49.180 --> 00:39:51.400
We get tons of requests,
hey, we need some data

00:39:51.400 --> 00:39:53.590
but we legally can't get it.

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:54.760
Can you analyze this?

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:56.920
We want some spatial analysis.

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:58.810
But I was just
thinking how cool it

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:02.770
would be if we had a local
nonprofit with the skill

00:40:02.770 --> 00:40:05.260
that your organization has.

00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:09.670
So I was wondering if you've
ever had a chance to collab

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:11.500
with local public
health agencies

00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:13.840
or academic
institutions and what

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:16.690
that relationship was like.

00:40:16.690 --> 00:40:17.920
Yeah.

00:40:17.920 --> 00:40:20.830
And I'm really
encouraged to hear

00:40:20.830 --> 00:40:22.630
that some of the
local governments

00:40:22.630 --> 00:40:26.260
are starting to get that
skill set in-house because I

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:28.240
don't think that's very common.

00:40:28.240 --> 00:40:30.070
So that's awesome to hear that.

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:33.790
We work closely with a lot
of county governments, city

00:40:33.790 --> 00:40:40.300
governments, universities,
definitely UNCW, NC State, UNC,

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:42.710
some folks at Duke and UNC
Charlotte, and some others.

00:40:42.710 --> 00:40:46.940
But to be honest, at
first, especially--

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:50.590
The county-- the local
governments are always all in.

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:51.730
They're like, yes, please.

00:40:51.730 --> 00:40:52.900
Help us.

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:55.075
Help us figure this out.

00:40:55.075 --> 00:40:57.320
There's been some
resistance at first

00:40:57.320 --> 00:40:58.570
from some of the universities.

00:40:58.570 --> 00:41:00.970
They're just like, who's
this plucky little nonprofit

00:41:00.970 --> 00:41:02.678
who's coming in here
and acting like they

00:41:02.678 --> 00:41:04.302
can do this data science work?

00:41:04.302 --> 00:41:06.010
And then when they
see like, oh, actually

00:41:06.010 --> 00:41:08.230
they've got some
people on staff who

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:09.970
have some credentials
and some experience

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:11.530
and know what
they're doing, then

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:13.907
their tune changes a little bit.

00:41:13.907 --> 00:41:18.610
But a lot of times--
one project we worked on

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:23.170
was there's a county
in North Carolina that

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:27.370
should be in the MSA
that Wilmington's in.

00:41:27.370 --> 00:41:29.750
But because there's a
river between the two,

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:34.100
there's a bridge, it's actually
in the Myrtle Beach MSA.

00:41:34.100 --> 00:41:38.020
So we did some work with the
Brunswick County government

00:41:38.020 --> 00:41:42.670
because their thing was,
look, 60% of our people

00:41:42.670 --> 00:41:45.280
are working in that
county right there,

00:41:45.280 --> 00:41:47.583
but we're in this other
MSA in South Carolina who

00:41:47.583 --> 00:41:48.500
doesn't care about us.

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:50.600
We don't get any support
from the funding there.

00:41:50.600 --> 00:41:52.720
So we're in this
weird ambiguous limbo

00:41:52.720 --> 00:41:58.030
space where can you help us make
the case to the Census Bureau

00:41:58.030 --> 00:42:00.110
that we should be in this MSA?

00:42:00.110 --> 00:42:02.920
So we compiled all
this data and basically

00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:06.130
found all these commuting
patterns and residency

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:09.940
patterns and everything else
that basically showed this

00:42:09.940 --> 00:42:12.070
belongs in this other county.

00:42:12.070 --> 00:42:13.870
TBD whether the Census
Bureau is actually

00:42:13.870 --> 00:42:16.370
going to listen to us because
we haven't got that result yet

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:19.990
back, but that's an
example of one of those

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:21.040
projects that we did.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:27.460
Awesome.

00:42:27.460 --> 00:42:29.410
Thank you.

00:42:29.410 --> 00:42:32.710
Jamie, I see you had asked a
question a bit earlier as well.

00:42:32.710 --> 00:42:35.320
Do you want to jump in next?

00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:37.780
And then you all for all
these great questions too.

00:42:41.660 --> 00:42:44.190
JAMIE SINTON: Thanks
for the opportunity.

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:45.997
I think I'm getting
the sense that I

00:42:45.997 --> 00:42:48.080
may be in the minority in
that data science is not

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:49.890
my primary work.

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:53.390
And so I'm trying to know
enough to be dangerous

00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:55.850
about data science just to
interact effectively with you

00:42:55.850 --> 00:42:57.050
guys.

00:42:57.050 --> 00:43:01.640
And so I'm curious when
you sit down with a client,

00:43:01.640 --> 00:43:05.900
like a university, what
expectations do you set

00:43:05.900 --> 00:43:09.290
or what deliverable
do you talk about?

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:13.430
Yeah.

00:43:13.430 --> 00:43:16.250
A lot of times it's just
knowledge sharing and data

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:17.660
sharing at first.

00:43:17.660 --> 00:43:21.800
It's, hey, we'd love to
work with your organization.

00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:23.960
Because a lot of
times universities

00:43:23.960 --> 00:43:28.100
are the path to opening
up greater data access.

00:43:28.100 --> 00:43:30.810
There's financial
institutions, for example,

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:36.350
who are way more willing
to give UNC Chapel

00:43:36.350 --> 00:43:40.370
Hill a big pile of people's
credit card transactions

00:43:40.370 --> 00:43:44.240
than they are my team of
nonprofit data scientists six

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:48.170
folks sitting on the
coast of North Carolina.

00:43:48.170 --> 00:43:51.830
So a lot of times that
conversation is, how can--

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:55.568
we've got a gap to fill
projects that are maybe

00:43:55.568 --> 00:43:57.860
too small for you because a
lot of-- these universities

00:43:57.860 --> 00:44:02.810
don't also want to sit next
to Cause for Pause and K9s

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:06.015
for Veterans and help them,
not because they don't want to,

00:44:06.015 --> 00:44:08.390
but because they have bigger
projects they're working on.

00:44:08.390 --> 00:44:11.640
Blue Cross Blue Shield
and other large projects.

00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:15.740
So we're filling a need
that you can't fill,

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:18.570
but we need resources
that you have.

00:44:18.570 --> 00:44:21.290
So we also have a
couple of projects

00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:25.040
where we'll enlist the research
arm of these facilities

00:44:25.040 --> 00:44:30.670
because love universities,
love research, love academia.

00:44:30.670 --> 00:44:36.793
Things move very slowly in the
university world and too slowly

00:44:36.793 --> 00:44:39.085
for a lot of the organizations
that we're working with.

00:44:41.780 --> 00:44:44.330
So yeah, that's where
we see that lane is.

00:44:44.330 --> 00:44:47.720
When it comes down to
doing a longitudinal study,

00:44:47.720 --> 00:44:49.490
we'll kick it over to you UNCW.

00:44:49.490 --> 00:44:55.110
But right now we just need
to do some basic analysis.

00:44:55.110 --> 00:44:59.100
JAMIE SINTON: And
so you're imagining

00:44:59.100 --> 00:45:05.160
taking the place of a university
statistician data management

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:08.190
person or you're coming
in with your own project

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:11.440
and asking the
university for data.

00:45:11.440 --> 00:45:13.630
It's usually more
the second route.

00:45:13.630 --> 00:45:14.130
Like we--

00:45:14.130 --> 00:45:15.005
JAMIE SINTON: Gotcha.

00:45:15.005 --> 00:45:15.940
We're not really--

00:45:15.940 --> 00:45:20.650
yeah, we're filling-- so a lot
of times, all these counties

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:23.710
have to fill out these community
health assessments every year

00:45:23.710 --> 00:45:26.350
or every three years,
I believe it is.

00:45:26.350 --> 00:45:28.870
They're mandated by law to
do these community health

00:45:28.870 --> 00:45:30.400
assessments.

00:45:30.400 --> 00:45:33.160
The universities are totally
capable of doing them,

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:37.150
but they bid for them.

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:40.390
And what you end up with is UNC
has done these and NC State's

00:45:40.390 --> 00:45:42.010
done these and ECU
has done these.

00:45:42.010 --> 00:45:44.410
And now it's really
hard for these counties

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:47.170
to compare how they're doing
against these other counties

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:49.900
because this other county has
a completely different format,

00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:51.970
completely different
metrics they're focused on.

00:45:51.970 --> 00:45:55.450
So one of the things
we've pushed on

00:45:55.450 --> 00:45:59.980
is, how can we help you align
on a common set of metrics

00:45:59.980 --> 00:46:02.170
so that when you're doing
it or you're doing it

00:46:02.170 --> 00:46:04.720
or you're doing it
Duke or whoever,

00:46:04.720 --> 00:46:06.735
we all are at least looking at--

00:46:06.735 --> 00:46:08.860
You're still going to have
room for your own flavor

00:46:08.860 --> 00:46:10.318
in your own research
and academics,

00:46:10.318 --> 00:46:12.760
but can we all at least agree
that these 10 metrics will

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:16.210
be in every single report,
measured the same way,

00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:18.430
talked about the
same way so that it

00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:20.860
allows us some level
of knowledge sharing

00:46:20.860 --> 00:46:21.550
and comparison.

00:46:21.550 --> 00:46:24.010
Like that's something
that we can--

00:46:24.010 --> 00:46:26.500
a recommendation we can
make as an outside entity

00:46:26.500 --> 00:46:30.650
that none of them are going
to make to each other.

00:46:30.650 --> 00:46:33.665
JAMIE SINTON: Interesting.

00:46:33.665 --> 00:46:37.280
Toyin, I see you
shared a link in the chat

00:46:37.280 --> 00:46:39.830
and just wanted to see
if you wanted to share

00:46:39.830 --> 00:46:43.210
a bit about what that is.

00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:44.590
TOYIN OLA: Certainly.

00:46:44.590 --> 00:46:47.530
So the Census Open
Innovation Lab,

00:46:47.530 --> 00:46:50.710
which is a subset of
the Census Bureau,

00:46:50.710 --> 00:46:53.410
has been doing a project
called The Opportunity

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:55.540
Project, or TOP,
for several years

00:46:55.540 --> 00:46:59.470
where they partner with
organizations in order

00:46:59.470 --> 00:47:03.740
to solve real-world
problems with open data.

00:47:03.740 --> 00:47:06.443
It sounds like
Cape Fear is really

00:47:06.443 --> 00:47:08.860
pushing the boundaries of what
can be done with open data,

00:47:08.860 --> 00:47:11.620
but that would be a way
to interface directly

00:47:11.620 --> 00:47:14.110
with government agencies
to use the data to solve

00:47:14.110 --> 00:47:17.410
problems and develop
data products to help

00:47:17.410 --> 00:47:19.540
local communities.

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:21.400
They did have a summit
a couple of weeks

00:47:21.400 --> 00:47:24.970
ago that was streamed to
YouTube that gives you

00:47:24.970 --> 00:47:26.830
an idea of what's been done.

00:47:26.830 --> 00:47:29.950
But the 2023 process is
getting ready to kick off

00:47:29.950 --> 00:47:33.765
in a couple of months so
it might be of interest.

00:47:33.765 --> 00:47:35.140
Oh, I hadn't
seen that one yet.

00:47:35.140 --> 00:47:38.280
I'm going to put
it on my list here.

00:47:38.280 --> 00:47:38.780
Yeah.

00:47:38.780 --> 00:47:40.940
Thank you.

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:45.770
Nick, I was remembering that we
were first talking on LinkedIn

00:47:45.770 --> 00:47:47.840
about Tidy Tuesday.

00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:49.790
And I was curious
if that's something

00:47:49.790 --> 00:47:52.910
that the team is
starting to look at now

00:47:52.910 --> 00:47:58.220
or if you were maybe sharing
data for a Tidy Tuesday project

00:47:58.220 --> 00:48:01.130
and maybe other open
source initiatives where

00:48:01.130 --> 00:48:03.150
people could get involved.

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:03.650
Yeah.

00:48:03.650 --> 00:48:06.670
So as a user, I've done Tidy
Tuesday a couple of times.

00:48:06.670 --> 00:48:12.710
I think I did one on slave
migration, one on tennis stars,

00:48:12.710 --> 00:48:16.400
maybe one on a couple
of other random things.

00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:17.960
I think it's just
such a cool project

00:48:17.960 --> 00:48:19.550
to just everyone
rally around a data

00:48:19.550 --> 00:48:24.300
set and code for a couple
of hours on a Tuesday.

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:28.820
I'm trying to think about
how to wrangle our data

00:48:28.820 --> 00:48:33.230
platform into something
that would be not too

00:48:33.230 --> 00:48:37.160
overwhelming for that because
we've got this big SQL

00:48:37.160 --> 00:48:38.180
database that has--

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:40.800
I think it's got 170,
180 tables in it.

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:42.800
Obviously, we don't want
to just say like, here,

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:44.090
play around in this big--

00:48:44.090 --> 00:48:45.610
because people
will just get lost.

00:48:45.610 --> 00:48:49.280
So can we create
like a CSV that we've

00:48:49.280 --> 00:48:51.530
joined three of
these tables together

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:54.740
and say like, all right,
you've got food insecurity,

00:48:54.740 --> 00:48:57.080
social vulnerability
index from the CDC,

00:48:57.080 --> 00:49:00.510
and poverty at a
census tract level.

00:49:00.510 --> 00:49:01.010
Go.

00:49:01.010 --> 00:49:03.440
What can you do?

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:05.030
So yeah, we're
definitely going to put

00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:09.320
a submission in just
how we provide something

00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:15.060
that's like the right scale
for an activity of that size.

00:49:15.060 --> 00:49:16.680
Cool.

00:49:16.680 --> 00:49:19.420
Dan, I see you had a question.

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:22.020
Can I have you jump in next?

00:49:22.020 --> 00:49:23.250
DAN SISCO: Yeah, sure.

00:49:23.250 --> 00:49:27.630
I just wanted to say awesome,
kudos for still teaching.

00:49:27.630 --> 00:49:30.720
I love it when people bring
their experiences in the field

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:31.685
right to the classroom.

00:49:31.685 --> 00:49:33.060
I was wondering
if you could talk

00:49:33.060 --> 00:49:36.030
at all about why you
chose to keep doing that

00:49:36.030 --> 00:49:39.470
and what rewards you see.

00:49:39.470 --> 00:49:39.980
Yeah.

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:42.540
I mean, if I'm being honest,
it wasn't really a choice.

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:45.620
I think people who
go into teaching

00:49:45.620 --> 00:49:48.812
have a certain personality
where it's just,

00:49:48.812 --> 00:49:50.270
I was going to be
teaching wherever

00:49:50.270 --> 00:49:51.575
I was going to be at anyways.

00:49:54.440 --> 00:49:57.230
Also, I think I learn that way.

00:49:57.230 --> 00:49:59.240
Some people learn
best through teaching.

00:49:59.240 --> 00:50:04.070
And when I have to explain
something three different ways,

00:50:04.070 --> 00:50:06.860
it just cements it my
understanding of it

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:10.200
that much better in my head.

00:50:10.200 --> 00:50:12.320
And so I think that's
the thing that I really

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:15.530
enjoy about teaching, is
like if you know something,

00:50:15.530 --> 00:50:18.108
you can always tell when
somebody really knows something

00:50:18.108 --> 00:50:20.150
if they can explain it a
bunch of different ways.

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:22.110
If you ask somebody
to explain something

00:50:22.110 --> 00:50:24.110
and then they say it and you're
like, I'm still not getting it,

00:50:24.110 --> 00:50:25.820
and they just like say
the same thing over again,

00:50:25.820 --> 00:50:27.487
it's like maybe you
don't get it either.

00:50:27.487 --> 00:50:29.510
Like both of us, I
think, don't get it.

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:32.357
But it's like, if they can
use different analogies,

00:50:32.357 --> 00:50:33.440
then it's like, all right.

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:34.890
Yeah, I got it.

00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:38.220
So I think that is just
part of how my brain works.

00:50:38.220 --> 00:50:42.290
And I just enjoy interacting
with people that way too.

00:50:48.940 --> 00:50:51.110
Niall, I see you have
a question as well.

00:50:51.110 --> 00:50:53.230
Do you want to jump in?

00:50:53.230 --> 00:50:53.730
Sure.

00:50:53.730 --> 00:50:54.420
Thanks.

00:50:54.420 --> 00:50:58.290
So we get pushback
sometimes for our projects

00:50:58.290 --> 00:51:01.300
not being data sciencey enough.

00:51:01.300 --> 00:51:03.270
And so I'm curious--

00:51:03.270 --> 00:51:05.400
and some of the stuff
you've described obviously

00:51:05.400 --> 00:51:09.390
is more advanced, some of it
sounds fairly descriptive.

00:51:09.390 --> 00:51:12.920
I'm curious what
those proportions are

00:51:12.920 --> 00:51:14.670
and how much that
influences your decision

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:17.000
to take on a project as a team.

00:51:17.000 --> 00:51:17.500
Yeah.

00:51:17.500 --> 00:51:22.050
Is that pushback from staff?

00:51:22.050 --> 00:51:22.800
It's both.

00:51:22.800 --> 00:51:24.010
It's both.

00:51:24.010 --> 00:51:26.655
If people are like, that's
not a predictive model, man.

00:51:26.655 --> 00:51:28.280
Why are you spending
your time on that?

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:30.540
I was like, well, it's
what was needed, right?

00:51:30.540 --> 00:51:31.305
Right.

00:51:31.305 --> 00:51:33.900
I think those conversations
are easier for me

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:36.000
when it's somebody pushing
back and saying like,

00:51:36.000 --> 00:51:37.530
this model's not
complicated enough.

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:39.780
I'm like, well, I can build
the support vector machine

00:51:39.780 --> 00:51:41.490
to do what this
logistic regression does

00:51:41.490 --> 00:51:43.920
if you want me
to, but it's going

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:47.550
to cost you another five weeks
of work and it's going to--

00:51:47.550 --> 00:51:50.160
the R squared is going to be
just the same or whatever.

00:51:50.160 --> 00:51:51.840
So I think that
conversation is easier.

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:55.620
The thing that I think--

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:57.880
I really struggled with when
I was building this team.

00:51:57.880 --> 00:51:59.490
So I was the first
data scientist.

00:51:59.490 --> 00:52:02.850
They brought me on
in April of 2020

00:52:02.850 --> 00:52:04.620
and they're basically
like, hey, we

00:52:04.620 --> 00:52:07.080
want to do this cool data thing,
this nonprofit data thing.

00:52:07.080 --> 00:52:07.590
I'm like, cool.

00:52:07.590 --> 00:52:08.760
What kind of data
sets do you have?

00:52:08.760 --> 00:52:09.720
They're like, we
don't have anything.

00:52:09.720 --> 00:52:10.600
I'm like, I'm in.

00:52:10.600 --> 00:52:12.032
Let's do it.

00:52:12.032 --> 00:52:13.740
And they're like, who
else is on my team?

00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:14.430
They're like, it's just you.

00:52:14.430 --> 00:52:15.300
I'm like, sweet.

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:16.620
I got it.

00:52:16.620 --> 00:52:18.370
So as I started
building the team out,

00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:20.550
I had to be really honest with
people I was bringing on board

00:52:20.550 --> 00:52:22.230
that if you're the
kind of person who

00:52:22.230 --> 00:52:26.700
wants to do NLP, bleeding edge,
TensorFlow, this kind of stuff,

00:52:26.700 --> 00:52:28.060
this is not the role for you.

00:52:28.060 --> 00:52:30.750
We're going to be doing a lot
of logistic regression, K-means,

00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:40.050
K-modes, summary, mean
equals whatever type stuff.

00:52:40.050 --> 00:52:42.750
So I just approached it by just
being really honest with them

00:52:42.750 --> 00:52:43.260
up front.

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:44.520
I said, but what
I can promise you

00:52:44.520 --> 00:52:46.140
is there's going to
be a lot of impact

00:52:46.140 --> 00:52:49.620
that we're going to make
because it's not a cop out to me

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:54.390
to say there's so much impact
that we can make with just

00:52:54.390 --> 00:52:56.130
descriptive
statistics and summary

00:52:56.130 --> 00:52:59.970
stuff right now in this
sector that we're just

00:52:59.970 --> 00:53:01.020
scratching the surface.

00:53:01.020 --> 00:53:03.750
By the time we get into some
of that advanced stuff--

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:05.040
and we were doing some of it.

00:53:05.040 --> 00:53:07.230
We've got some pretty
big projects where

00:53:07.230 --> 00:53:09.340
we've done some forecasting.

00:53:09.340 --> 00:53:14.418
We've done some
more modeling stuff.

00:53:14.418 --> 00:53:16.210
But I think that's how
we've approached it,

00:53:16.210 --> 00:53:19.090
is just been super honest about
like, this isn't that role.

00:53:19.090 --> 00:53:21.220
It's not going to be a
Google engineer role.

00:53:24.262 --> 00:53:25.110
DAN SISCO: Thanks.

00:53:25.110 --> 00:53:25.610
Yeah.

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:32.280
I remember us talking about
that before in another hangout.

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:34.260
I was trying to remember
if it was Allen who

00:53:34.260 --> 00:53:37.940
was talking about it before.

00:53:37.940 --> 00:53:41.562
I see Adrian you had
asked a question.

00:53:41.562 --> 00:53:42.520
Do you want to jump in?

00:53:46.673 --> 00:53:47.590
ADRIAN LINDSEY: Sorry.

00:53:47.590 --> 00:53:49.040
Yeah.

00:53:49.040 --> 00:53:50.570
Eating my lunch,
but I'll jump in.

00:53:50.570 --> 00:53:51.530
No worries.

00:53:51.530 --> 00:53:52.638
I can read it too.

00:53:52.638 --> 00:53:53.430
ADRIAN LINDSEY: No.

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:54.158
No.

00:53:54.158 --> 00:53:54.950
I'll just stay on--

00:53:54.950 --> 00:53:59.150
I'll just stay off camera so
you don't have to watch me chew.

00:53:59.150 --> 00:54:01.940
You had mentioned a
handful of these projects

00:54:01.940 --> 00:54:05.450
where you were
able to really take

00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:10.080
advantage of these
open data packages that

00:54:10.080 --> 00:54:12.047
seemed to unlock
some of the questions

00:54:12.047 --> 00:54:13.380
that the nonprofits were asking.

00:54:13.380 --> 00:54:18.830
And I'm just curious how
much of those projects

00:54:18.830 --> 00:54:23.690
are shared, if you and your
team maintain a GitHub, if--

00:54:23.690 --> 00:54:26.750
I'd really like to
start connecting

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:31.460
some of those dots myself,
just intellectual curiosity.

00:54:31.460 --> 00:54:35.390
And I have tried to take
advantage of open sources

00:54:35.390 --> 00:54:39.800
in the past and I've had
a few walls so seeing some

00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:45.450
that actually go off the ground
would be really interesting.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:46.140
Yeah.

00:54:46.140 --> 00:54:49.380
We do have a GitHub,
of course, but it

00:54:49.380 --> 00:54:51.390
is private to our
organization right now.

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:57.810
Not because we want to
hoard the information,

00:54:57.810 --> 00:55:00.420
but really just because
we're just not at a state

00:55:00.420 --> 00:55:02.165
where-- it's messy in there.

00:55:02.165 --> 00:55:03.540
We've got some
cleaning up to do.

00:55:03.540 --> 00:55:06.780
We've been going so fast
for a couple of years

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:09.120
that a lot of stuff
needs to be organized.

00:55:09.120 --> 00:55:11.360
And we really need
to figure out too

00:55:11.360 --> 00:55:16.540
which pieces we're able
to elevate to the public.

00:55:16.540 --> 00:55:18.330
We do have a desire to be--

00:55:18.330 --> 00:55:22.470
I just put the link to our
community data platform,

00:55:22.470 --> 00:55:25.240
didn't hyperlink for
some reason, in the chat.

00:55:25.240 --> 00:55:30.060
But that's a public
interface into our--

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:32.627
it's into the back end
of the SQL database.

00:55:32.627 --> 00:55:33.960
So go and click around in there.

00:55:33.960 --> 00:55:35.752
You can see some of
the sources of the data

00:55:35.752 --> 00:55:37.080
that we're working with.

00:55:37.080 --> 00:55:40.440
But the thing is, there's
really a couple of partitions

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:42.750
that we have in our
community data platform.

00:55:42.750 --> 00:55:45.750
There's what you can see
through this link, which

00:55:45.750 --> 00:55:48.430
is Census Bureau, CDC,
all that kind of stuff.

00:55:48.430 --> 00:55:50.790
But then there's some
